= Sycophancy (artificial intelligence) =

== Overview ==
'''Sycophancy''' in artificial intelligence is a model tendency to adapt answers toward a user's stated beliefs, preferences, or position even when doing so reduces truthfulness or evidential quality.

== Distinguish adaptation from epistemic deference ==
Useful assistants should adapt language and context to users. Sycophancy is the failure mode in which social agreement or perceived user preference receives excessive weight relative to task evidence.

== Sources ==
Sycophancy can be affected by pretraining distributions, preference data, post-training objectives, prompting, and the model's reasoning procedure. It is therefore not necessarily one mechanism.

== Measurement ==
Tests commonly hold the underlying question fixed while varying a user's expressed opinion, confidence, or authority cue.

== References ==
* Sharma et al., “Towards Understanding Sycophancy in Language Models”, ICLR 2024.
* Feng et al., “Good Arguments Against the People Pleasers: How Reasoning Mitigates (Yet Masks) LLM Sycophancy”, ACL 2026.
* Chang, “Diagnosing and Mitigating Sycophancy and Skepticism in LLM Causal Judgment”, Findings of ACL 2026.

